WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
A session ends, the context clears, and everything the model learned goes with it.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.520
So where do the facts you can't lose actually live?

3
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:15.279
This lesson walks through the memory tool, how a model saves facts that outlast a single conversation.

4
00:00:15.279 --> 00:00:21.879
We'll see how it works, the mindset behind it, and how it differs from memory inside the chat.

5
00:00:21.879 --> 00:00:27.000
Ask what a model remembers after you close the tab, and by default the answer is nothing.

6
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.200
The memory tool changes that.

7
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:35.799
The scratchpad file habit you saw earlier in the course is this same idea, now a first class tool.

8
00:00:35.799 --> 00:00:42.040
It's a tool the model uses to read and write files under a dedicated memory path, so facts

9
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.680
persist across separate conversations.

10
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.779
Client side is the key idea behind it.

11
00:00:47.779 --> 00:00:54.319
The model requests an action, and your own application actually performs it against storage you control.

12
00:00:54.319 --> 00:00:57.000
In context memory is the contrast.

13
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.040
That's anything the model knows, purely because it's sitting in the current transcript.

14
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:08.819
It feels like memory, but it vanishes the moment that context is cleared or summarized.

15
00:01:08.819 --> 00:01:13.080
The whole lesson turns on the gap between the first and the last.

16
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.120
So how does the memory tool actually work?

17
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.559
The flow is a request and a response.

18
00:01:18.559 --> 00:01:23.180
The model decides it wants to do something with memory, say view the folder or create

19
00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.940
a file, and it issues that request.

20
00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:31.019
Your application receives it, runs the operation against whatever storage you've set up, and

21
00:01:31.019 --> 00:01:33.900
returns the result back to the model.

22
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.699
The files themselves live under a memory path that you fully control.

23
00:01:38.699 --> 00:01:43.760
It could be a folder on disk, or keys in a database, whatever fits your system.

24
00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.500
The upshot is that the model can build up notes over many turns and many sessions without

25
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.980
having to hold all of that in its context at once.

26
00:01:53.019 --> 00:01:56.860
It reads back what it needs when it needs it.

27
00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.260
What can it actually do with those files?

28
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.339
Three things at a foundations level.

29
00:02:01.339 --> 00:02:06.059
It can read, meaning it views the memory folder and opens files to recall what an earlier

30
00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:07.699
session wrote down.

31
00:02:07.699 --> 00:02:13.179
It can write, creating, or editing files to save facts, progress, and decisions that are

32
00:02:13.179 --> 00:02:14.619
worth keeping around.

33
00:02:14.619 --> 00:02:19.679
And it can organize, renaming, or deleting files so the store stays coherent instead

34
00:02:19.679 --> 00:02:21.820
of turning into a junk drawer.

35
00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:25.820
It can also map to the same operations you'd expect on any set of files.

36
00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:30.500
The important shift in thinking is that the model is now an active caretaker of a durable

37
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:36.580
notebook, not just a talker that forgets everything the instant the conversation ends.

38
00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:41.699
The tool ships with a specific design instruction, and it's worth taking to heart.

39
00:02:41.699 --> 00:02:44.940
The mindset is assume interruption.

40
00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:49.660
Assume the context window could reset at any moment, for any reason.

41
00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:55.619
Which means anything you haven't written to memory is at risk of being lost the instant that happens.

42
00:02:55.619 --> 00:02:58.940
The practical rule that follows is simple.

43
00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:03.660
Check your memory first before doing anything else, and then record your progress as you

44
00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.580
go, rather than waiting until the end.

45
00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.539
When you design an agent this way, an interruption stops being a disaster.

46
00:03:11.539 --> 00:03:14.460
It becomes a resume instead of a restart.

47
00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:19.619
That single shift in assumption is what makes long-running work robust.

48
00:03:19.619 --> 00:03:24.940
Let's put this next to the scratchpad file, because that's the closest thing you've already seen.

49
00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:29.740
Both end up as durable text sitting outside the transcript, so on the surface they look

50
00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:31.300
like the same idea.

51
00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.580
The difference is, who does the work?

52
00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:38.500
A scratchpad only holds what you remembered to write, and it only helps you if you remembered

53
00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.100
to load it back in.

54
00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:43.300
The memory tool moves both halves onto the model.

55
00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:48.179
It decides a fact is worth keeping and issues the write itself, then reads back what it

56
00:03:48.179 --> 00:03:50.179
needs when it needs it.

57
00:03:50.179 --> 00:03:53.660
You supply the execution layer and the storage underneath.

58
00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:58.820
That shift is what makes it survive a reset you never planned for, which is exactly where

59
00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:03.139
a hand-maintained file tends to let you down.

60
00:04:03.139 --> 00:04:05.779
One last connection before we close.

61
00:04:05.779 --> 00:04:09.639
Compaction and the memory tool are partners, not rivals.

62
00:04:09.639 --> 00:04:14.580
Compaction keeps the active context small on its own, by summarizing the conversation

63
00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:16.579
so far server-side.

64
00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:21.260
The memory tool preserves exactly the kind of specifics a summary might drop.

65
00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:25.660
Put them together and you get long runs that don't blow past the window and don't lose

66
00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.440
the essentials either.

67
00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.260
The clean mental model is a division of labor.

68
00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:34.579
Let compaction handle the bulk of the history automatically, while the memory tool guards

69
00:04:34.579 --> 00:04:38.179
the handful of facts that absolutely have to survive.

70
00:04:38.179 --> 00:04:43.260
Lean context on one side, durable state on the other.

71
00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:47.980
When a session ends and nothing was written down, the next one starts from zero.

72
00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:51.579
A week-long project could lose an entire week.

73
00:04:51.579 --> 00:04:54.500
That's the failure the memory tool is aimed at.

74
00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:58.220
Assume the context can reset at any moment.

75
00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:03.339
Check the store before you start, and record progress as you go.

76
00:05:03.339 --> 00:05:08.059
Then an interruption costs you a read instead of a complete rebuild.

77
00:05:08.059 --> 00:05:09.420
That does it for this lesson.

78
00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:10.540
I'll see you in the next one.

